<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prilep</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prilep is a city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelagonia">Pelagonia</a> plain of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>. Above it stand the ruins of the Towers of Marko, the fortress of the 14th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vukasin-mrnjavcevic">King Vukašin</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-marko">Prince Marko</a>, who ruled the Kingdom of Prilep after the break-up of the Serbian Empire until the Ottoman conquest. The town is a centre of tobacco growing.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prilep.jpg" alt="Prilep" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tomica · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panorama_of_Prilep,_2019.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Prilep lies in the northern part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelagonia">Pelagonia</a> plain, in the south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>, 74 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a> and 44 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bitola">Bitola</a>. In antiquity the region belonged to Pelagonia and was annexed to the Macedonian kingdom during the 4th century BCE. Near the village of Čepigovo are the ruins of the ancient city of Styberra, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> razed in 268 though it remained partly inhabited. The name first appears in Greek as Prilapos in 1014, and derives from Old Slavic words meaning stuck on the rock, after the hilltop above the town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1014 Tsar Samuel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> was said to have had a heart attack at Prilep on seeing thousands of his soldiers blinded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kleidion">Battle of Kleidion</a>. In 1041 the Byzantine emperor Michael IV defeated Petar Delyan's rebellion in the region. Byzantium lost the town to the Second Bulgarian Empire, but retook it. Serbian King Dušan took Prilep in 1334, and after 1365 it belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vukasin-mrnjavcevic">King Vukašin</a>, co-ruler with Dušan's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-uros-v">Stefan Uroš V</a>. After Vukašin's death in 1371 his son Marko ruled it, and rebuilt the citadel into an important stronghold.</p><p>Prilep became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in 1395 and stayed so until 1913, when the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> annexed it. Its bazaar began to develop in the 18th century, and one of the largest annual fairs in Macedonia was held there in the middle of the 19th. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> occupied the town from 17 November 1915 to 25 September 1918. It then joined the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a> and, from 1929, the Vardar Banovina. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> it was occupied by the German Army on 8 April 1941 and by the Bulgarian Army on 26 April 1941, and local partisans attacked the police station on 11 October 1941. Communist partisans retook it definitively on 3 November 1944.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Prilep is a regional centre of tobacco growing and also produces white marble. The Markovi Kuli, the ruins of the fortress above the town, are among its medieval sites, along with the Treskavec Monastery, built in the 12th century in the mountains north of the town, which has frescoes of the 14th and 15th centuries. The dialect of Prilep, with those of Veles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bitola">Bitola</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohrid">Ohrid</a>, was chosen as a basis of the standard Macedonian language after the Socialist Republic of Macedonia was formed within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Prilep” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1333 Dušan attacked the Byzantine Empire of Andronikos III Palaiologos with the help of the Byzantine general Syrgian, taking Ohrid, <b>Prilep</b> and Kastoria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/serbian-empire">Serbian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Berziti were among the Slavic groups that settled in Macedonia in the 7th century, in the region around Ohrid, <b>Prilep</b> and Bitola.</blockquote><a href="/state/berziti">Berziti</a></li><li><blockquote>Vukašin Mrnjavčević, a Serbian noble, was crowned king in 1365 as co-ruler with Emperor Uroš V of the Serbian Empire, ruling lands in western Macedonia from <b>Prilep</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-prilep">Kingdom of Prilep</a></li></ul></section>
